@page "/interop"

<CatHead Title="From C#" Tier="@CatHead.Site">
    <code>ISednaUi</code> is the typed way into the <a href="/script">script</a> from a component:
    every member is one JavaScript call, and none of them adds behaviour the script does not
    already have. This page is the members that are easiest to get wrong; the rest are on the
    page of the thing they drive.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-braces-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Register it once, and never call it while prerendering</span>
        <code>builder.Services.AddSednaUi()</code>, then <code>@@inject ISednaUi Ui</code>. Every
        member is a JavaScript call, so <strong>none of them can run during prerendering</strong>
        — call them from an event handler, or from
        <code>OnAfterRenderAsync(firstRender: true)</code>. The wrappers do not swallow that
        exception.
    </div>
</div>

<CatSnippet Name="Interop/AfterFirstRender.txt" Title="Something to say before the circuit is up">
    <p>
        A toast decided in <code>OnInitializedAsync</code> — a record that failed to load — cannot be
        shown there: the page is still prerendering. Keep the outcome as state, and say it once the
        first interactive render has happened. That is the whole recipe; a flag on
        <code>ISednaUi</code> would only move the same <code>if</code> somewhere harder to see.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Interop/FromTheLayout.txt" Title="Once per circuit, and after every navigation">
    <p>
        The layout outlives every page, so it is where the app says what holds for the whole session:
        <code>ConfigureAsync</code> pushes the options given to <code>AddSednaUi</code>, and
        <code>RegisterSearchAsync</code> and <code>RegisterCommandsAsync</code> hand over the header search's index
        and the palette's commands, each replacing the last list. A navigation in Blazor is not a page load, so
        nothing the browser would reset is reset: <code>CloseSearchAsync</code>, <code>ClosePaletteAsync</code>,
        <code>CloseMenusAsync</code> and <code>HideTipsAsync</code> put away what belonged to the last page, and
        <code>ScrollPageTopAsync</code> starts the new one at its top. This site's own layout does the same.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.ToastsFromCsharp" Title="Toasts" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>ToastAsync</code> takes the message and a <code>ToastKind</code>, whose members are the
        CSS modifier suffixes, so <code>ToastKind.Go</code> and <code>.toast--go</code> cannot drift
        apart. A toast is one of the two pieces of UI the library draws for you, because a line of
        text has nothing to author. It returns a <a href="/toast#work-that-ends-later">handle</a>
        for dismissing or replacing it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.MenuAtPointFromCsharp" Title="A menu at the pointer, opened by the app" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; right-click a tile.</strong> <code>OpenMenuAtAsync</code> opens a
        <code>.menu[popover]</code> you wrote at the pointer, as
        <a href="/menu"><code>data-menu-context</code></a> does, for a menu your code decides to open:
        after selecting the record, checking the reader's rights, or not at all. It takes
        <code>MouseEventArgs.ClientX</code> and <code>ClientY</code> and the id of the element the
        menu is about, which carries <code>data-menu-context-target</code> while it is open; a scroll
        that moves that element closes the menu. Placement, flipping at the screen's edges,
        <kbd>Escape</kbd> and an outside click are the same as for a right-click.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.SettingsFromCsharp" Title="Appearance settings" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>ISednaSettings</code> is the applied settings as state, with a <code>Changed</code>
        event — where <code>ISednaUi.LoadSettingsAsync</code> only answers what they are now, which
        goes stale when a reader on “System” changes their OS between light and dark. Call
        <code>StartAsync</code> from <code>OnAfterRenderAsync(firstRender)</code>, and check
        <code>IsLive</code> before drawing a toggle, or someone who chose light sees “dark” selected
        for one frame.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.TimeZoneFromCsharp" Title="The browser's time zone" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — this is your own zone.</strong> <code>ISednaUi.GetTimeZoneAsync()</code> reads
        <code>Intl</code> through the circuit and returns the IANA id, so a Blazor Server app gets the
        zone on the <em>first</em> session in a new browser — where the
        <a href="/script">cookie <code>boot.js</code> writes</a> is still absent and a navigation is
        not an HTTP request. The two are the same answer at two moments: the cookie serves the
        first server render, this serves the circuit. It is interop, so call it from
        <code>OnAfterRenderAsync(firstRender: true)</code>, and keep the configured fallback for the
        render before it answers.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.SettingsPanelFromCsharp" Title="A settings panel" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; these are this site's settings.</strong> <code>SetThemeAsync</code> takes a theme
        registered with <code>AddSednaUi</code>; <code>SetDensityAsync</code> takes <code>comfortable</code>,
        <code>compact</code> or <code>touch</code>; <code>SetColourBlindAsync</code> switches the colour-vision
        palette; <code>SetLanguageAsync</code> sets the document's <code>lang</code>, for the app to render its own
        words in; and <code>SetDirectionAsync</code> takes <code>ltr</code> or <code>rtl</code>. Each stores the
        choice and lets <code>Changed</code> come back, so the controls are drawn from <code>Current</code> and
        nothing else.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>ISednaUi</code> reaches the same settings without listening. <code>LoadSettingsAsync</code> is right
        for a value taken once, such as what a problem report attaches. <code>SaveSettingAsync</code> stores one
        key &mdash; <code>theme</code>, <code>variant</code>, <code>cvd</code>, <code>density</code>,
        <code>lang</code>, or an app's own name listed in the boot script's <code>data-app-attrs</code> &mdash; and
        the panel follows it all the same.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.BrowserFromCsharp" Title="The browser, from C#" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> The small calls a component cannot make by itself. <code>CopyTextAsync</code> copies
        a value the server has only just made, where <a href="/script"><code>data-copy</code></a> needs a value
        written in the markup, and answers whether the browser allowed it. <code>ViewportWidthAsync</code> is for a
        decision the server has to make &mdash; how many lines to fetch &mdash; and never for layout, which the
        stylesheet's breakpoints keep. <code>GetItemAsync</code> and <code>SetItemAsync</code> read and write
        <code>localStorage</code> under the key exactly as given, with no prefix.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>SelectTabAsync</code> selects a <code>data-tabs</code> tab by the id of the panel it controls, as a
        click would; in a <code>data-tabs="managed"</code> tablist it clicks the tab, and the app's handler
        answers. <code>OpenTabAsync</code> opens an address in a new tab with <code>noopener</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.DesktopNotifyFromCsharp" Title="Desktop notifications" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; your browser asks first.</strong> <code>RequestNotifyAsync</code> asks for permission,
        and only works from a click: a browser refuses the question outside a user gesture.
        <code>NotifyAsync</code> shows a notification by the system, outside the page, and
        <code>PingAsync</code> plays a short tone, with no file to ship. Both are best effort &mdash; without
        permission nothing is shown &mdash; so say the same on the page, with a
        <a href="/toast">toast</a> or a status line.
    </p>
</CatExample>

